  ],
  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Credential Access — Steal Web Session Cookie", "technique": "T1539", "what_happened": "At 2026-05-13 09:14:07 CEST, Wazuh rule 5721 'Bearer Token Used from Different IP — No Login Event' fired. data.srcip=194.165.16.77 (external, AS215540 DataLine LLC, CIS region) presented a valid authenticated bearer token (JWT, sub=user_123, alice.jones@corp.local) against data.url=/api/v1/users/me on the web app host 10.108.57.177. The decisive anomaly: the SAME session token / session_id a7f2e9c8b3d6f1a4e0c2b5d8f3a1e4c7 had been in continuous use from internal host 192.168.1.45 until 08:55:33 the same morning — and there is NO /login or authentication event from 194.165.16.77 preceding the token use. A valid session simply materialised from a new external IP with no re-authentication. This is the canonical infostealer-log session-replay pattern (Redline/Raccoon/LummaC2 exfiltrate cookies + bearer tokens, the log is sold on a broker market, the buyer replays the token).", "chain_reason": "Wazuh rule 5721 [T1539] correlated data.http.user_agent + session identifier a7f2e9c8b3d6f1a4e0c2b5d8f3a1e4c7 being presented from data.srcip=194.165.16.77 with zero preceding authentication event from that srcip — same session identifier, two different source IPs, two different geolocations (internal corporate vs CIS data center), no re-auth. Impossible travel on the session identifier itself.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T09:14:07+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Initial Access — Acquire Access (replay stolen session)", "technique": "T1650", "what_happened": "During 09:14-09:18 CEST the same data.srcip=194.165.16.77 probed a series of authenticated API endpoints with the stolen JWT — data.url values /api/v1/users/me, /api/v1/profile, /api/v2/users/me, /portal/api/me, /api/auth/me, /api/v1/session — all with data.http.method=GET. Wazuh rule 5720 'Session Token Replay from New Geographic Location' [T1650] and rule 5722 'JWT Replay: iat/exp mismatch with source IP' [T1650] fired. The attacker is verifying the stolen token is still live (not revoked) before escalating. The token usage pattern — broad endpoint probing immediately on first contact, no UI navigation, no static-asset loads — is machine-driven validation, not a human browsing.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh rules 5720 + 5722 [T1650] correlated multiple data.url authenticated-endpoint GETs from data.srcip=194.165.16.77 carrying the same JWT bound to alice.jones@corp.local, within a 4-minute window, with the iat/exp claims of the JWT inconsistent with a session legitimately originating from that source IP/geo.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T09:14:07+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Discovery — Account & Permission Enumeration via API", "technique": "T1087", "what_happened": "During 09:18-09:24 CEST data.srcip=194.165.16.77 pivoted from token-validation to escalation. Using the still-valid stolen JWT it issued data.http.method=GET requests against enumeration and admin data.url endpoints — /api/v1/users, /api/v1/users?limit=500, /api/v1/users?role=admin, /api/v1/roles, /api/v1/groups, /api/v2/admin/accounts, /api/v1/permissions, then /admin, /admin/users, /admin/config, /api/v1/admin/users — followed by data export attempts on /api/v1/users/export, /api/v1/export/users.csv, /api/v1/config/secrets. Wazuh rule 31501 'User Enumeration via API Post-Auth' [T1087] fired. The attacker is mapping the tenant and reaching for bulk user data + secrets while the hijacked session is live.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh rule 31501 [T1087] correlated a burst of authenticated GET requests from data.srcip=194.165.16.77 against user/role/permission listing data.url endpoints plus /admin* paths plus *export* paths, all carrying the same hijacked alice.jones session, in a tight 6-minute window — discovery + collection behaviour following the session replay.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T09:18:33+0200"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Persistence — Unauthorized Token Refresh / Session Extension", "technique": "T1078", "what_happened": "During 09:24-09:28 CEST data.srcip=194.165.16.77 attempted to make its access persistent. It issued data.http.method=POST requests to OAuth2 / token endpoints — /oauth/token, /oauth2/token, /api/v1/auth/refresh, /connect/token — with a stolen refresh_token grant, then POSTed to JWT renewal endpoints /api/v1/auth/renew, /api/v1/auth/extend, and finally a data.http.method=PATCH to /api/v1/session/bind attempting to register 194.165.16.77 as a trusted_ip for session_id a7f2e9c8b3d6f1a4e0c2b5d8f3a1e4c7 — i.e. trying to defeat the geo-anomaly detection that caught it. Wazuh rule 31502 'Unauthorized Token Refresh Attempt' [T1078] fired. The intent is a self-sustaining session that survives the original token's expiry and no longer trips IP/geo anomaly rules.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh rule 31502 [T1078] correlated POST requests to /oauth/token, /oauth2/token, /api/v1/auth/refresh, /connect/token and PATCH /api/v1/session/bind from data.srcip=194.165.16.77 carrying the hijacked alice.jones credentials — refresh-token grant + IP-binding manipulation = an attempt to convert a one-shot stolen token into durable access.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T09:24:11+0200"}
  ],
